gen_2501_08735.py:65:        "O(layers^n) search and averaged 8.13 s, 82,276 recursive nodes, and "
README.md:24:The graph/exact-cover mechanical reference is minimum-column Algorithm X, worst-case `O(layers^n)`. Across eight shipping seeds it succeeded as expected but averaged 82,276 recursive nodes, 26,936,781 exact incidence tests, and 8.13 seconds (maximum 31.19 seconds). This gap—not a false Track A claim—is the benchmark: a no-tool solver has to discover the invariant and accurately extract 35 IDs. The paper's easy regimes are explicitly avoided: Theorem 12 makes bipartite diameter-at-most-3 instances polynomial and Theorem 15 does the same for radius at most 2. Our graphs use Theorem 23's radius-3/diameter-4 construction.
README.md:103:The tags intentionally make this distribution polynomial-time solvable; deleting or ignoring them changes the benchmark, while exposing precomputed differences makes it trivial. The 0/200,000 guess estimate is relative to the stated structure-aware prior—one gadget per A element—not a uniform prior over arbitrary ID lists, and it cannot rule out a tiny nonzero density. Algorithm X showed wide seed-to-seed variance (0.25 seconds on the G5 seed versus a 31.19-second maximum in G6), so the baseline is a measured distribution, not a per-instance lower bound. The four local attacks do not include a commercial CP-SAT/ILP solver or a dedicated exact-cover package; the in-module Algorithm X is the domain-standard substitute. `canonical_key` is exact for the generator's tested ID, gadget-order, affine-tag, and B/C-swap symmetries, but is not a complete general graph-isomorphism canonical form. Finally, all oracle conclusions remain blocked by the exhausted external key; the error transcripts are preserved verbatim.

